{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ויהי ביום כלות", :translit "vayehi b'yom kalot", :gloss "and it was on the day of completing"} {:he "חנוכת המזבח", :translit "chanukat ha-mizbei'ach", :gloss "dedication of the altar"} {:he "ברכת כהנים", :translit "birkat kohanim", :gloss "blessing of the priests"} {:he "חשמונאים", :translit "chashmona'im", :gloss "Hasmoneans"} {:he "חנוכה", :translit "chanukah", :gloss "dedication"} {:he "ה' יברך את עמו בשלום", :translit "Hashem yivarech et amo va-shalom", :gloss "May YHWH bless His people with peace"} {:he "שיר חנוכת הבית", :translit "shir chanukat ha-bayit", :gloss "A song for the dedication of the House"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhut parshiyot: juxtaposition of the altar dedication to the priestly blessing and to the Mishkan"}], :frame {:text "The priestly blessing of Numbers 6:24–26 immediately precedes the dedication of the altar in Numbers 7:1. Psalm 30 is titled 'A song for the dedication of the House' (shir chanukat ha-bayit, Psalm 30:1) and its closing verse reads 'YHWH bless His people with peace' — echoing the closing word of the priestly blessing, 'peace' (shalom, Numbers 6:26). The term chanukat thereby links the altar dedication, the Temple psalm, and the Hasmonean festival of Chanukah.", :sources ["Numbers 6:24–26" "Numbers 7:1" "Psalm 30:1" "Psalm 29:11"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ושמו את שמי ואני אברכם", :translit "vesamu et shmi va'ani avarchem", :gloss "and they shall place My name and I will bless them"} {:he "ויהי ביום כלות", :translit "vayehi b'yom kalot", :gloss "and it was on the day of completing"} {:he "בכל מקום אשר אזכיר את שמי אבא אליך וברכתיך", :translit "b'chol makom asher azkir et shmi ava eilecha u'veirachticha", :gloss "in every place where I mention My name I will come to you and bless you"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhut parshiyot: Numbers 6:27 juxtaposed to Numbers 7:1, read through the promise of Exodus 20:24"}], :frame {:text "Numbers 6:27 concludes the priestly blessing with 'and they shall place My name upon the children of Israel, and I will bless them.' Exodus 20:24 promises 'in every place where I cause My name to be mentioned, I will come to you and bless you.' By juxtaposing Numbers 6:27 to the dedication narrative of Numbers 7:1, the altar dedication is read as the fulfillment of that promise: the moment the priests invoke the name, God's blessing arrives.", :sources ["Numbers 6:27" "Numbers 7:1" "Exodus 20:24"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Semikhut parshiyot: juxtaposition of the altar dedication to the priestly blessing and to the Mishkan", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Semikhut parshiyot: Numbers 6:27 juxtaposed to Numbers 7:1, read through the promise of Exodus 20:24", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:27:28.864348968Z", :model "glm-5.2"}